WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.540
Hey everyone, welcome to Models and Agents Episode 100 on July 3, 2026.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.200
Let's see what shipped.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.880
Plenty of model releases and agent benchmarks moving today.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.940
That's Episode 100, a big milestone.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.540
Thank you for being here.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.260
Local browser agents just became practical and private.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.020
Webbrain runs entirely in Chrome or Firefox using your own models.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.800
Webbrain is a free MIT licensed AI browser agent

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.580
for Chrome and Firefox that reads pages, extracts data,

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.420
and automates multi-step tasks through ask and act modes.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.380
It runs on localmodels via llama.cpp or llama

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.380
for full privacy or connects to any cloud API when needed.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.200
The tool directly addresses the gap between demo agents

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.180
and usable daily automation by keeping all page processing inside the browser.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.220
Developers gain a concrete starting point for local browser

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.180
automation without sending sensitive page content to third parties.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.000
Watch how the community extends the ask and act modes

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.480
and whether it becomes the default base for custom local agents.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.780
This sits in the agents and tool use arc we tracked through Episode 99.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.700
The release moves the open question on long horizon reliability forward

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:24.500
by showing a working local first pattern that avoids third-party data exposure.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.480
Webbrain delivers an open source MIT licensed browser agent

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:34.360
that reads pages and automates multi-step tasks via ask and act modes.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.860
Simon Willison shipped a one-shot CLI coding agent built on his LLM library.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.960
Safety research introduced provenance guard and sigil to catch misalignment

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.340
before tool calls execute. Builders should watch how local first agents change

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.180
the privacy and reliability trade-offs this week.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.480
Rule Chef uses large language models only at training time

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.700
to synthesize human editable rules for classification,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.560
named Entity Recognition and Relation Extraction from task descriptions

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.900
and labeled examples. The approach bootstraps from any existing

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:15.340
models input output pairs and releases under Apache 2.0.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.680
Builders working on production natural language processing pipelines

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.280
should test whether the generated rules replace brittle prompt chains this week.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:33.780
Office comprehension bench evaluates large language models on native.docx.xlsx and .pptx files across

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.300
structural perception and expert level domain reasoning in 12 industries.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:45.160
Even frontier systems reach only 59.3% on the domain question and answer track.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.040
With deeper thinking yielding little gain while moving to higher product tiers

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:55.060
produces modest improvement. The benchmark releases the data set, tooling,

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.540
judge prompt and public leaderboard. Teams building document agents should add it to their

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:06.480
evaluation suite immediately. Tokenscope provides an interactive tool for decoder-based

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.420
large language models that exposes token level metrics, attention patterns and structural

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:18.540
information during code generation. It supports token replacement, counterfactual branching and

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:25.100
abstract syntax tree-aware aggregation. Researchers studying large language model code behavior

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:30.600
gain a practical way to explore alternative generation paths without post-hawk analysis.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:38.340
Cigil adds a verification step before AI agents sign or execute transactions, replacing blind

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:44.640
yes clicks with explicit review. The guardrail targets the common failure mode where agents propose

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:51.620
actions that deviate from user intent. Early users report it fits naturally into existing agent loops

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:58.540
without major refactoring. Teams shipping agent finance or crypto tools should evaluate it this week.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:05.120
Providence Guard formalizes misalignment detection as provenance analysis and blocks tool calls

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.000
unsupported by traceable evidence in the agent's context. On agent safety bench,

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:19.520
it cuts error rate on misaligned traces from 42.9% to 1.8% while lowering unnecessary interventions

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:26.380
on successful traces. The multi-stage pipeline works across 10 backbone large language models and

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:32.700
adds no statistically significant overhead on aligned actions. Agent developers gain a concrete,

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:40.320
auditable alternative to large language model as a judge guardrails. Simon Willison one shot a CLI

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.400
coding agent on top of his LLM Python library and documented the full fable experiment.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:52.540
The agent operates directly on local or remote models through the library's existing interface.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:59.280
Builders already using LLM can replicate the pattern in minutes and extend it for their own workflows.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:06.300
This release shows continued rapid iteration on simple library backed agents rather than new frameworks.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:12.780
The AI journal article catalogs concrete failure modes that appear only after agents leave

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:19.900
controlled demos, including state management drift. Tools schema mismatches surface once agents operate

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:26.900
outside sandbox test conditions. Compounding errors over long horizons create additional breakdowns

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:32.820
not visible in short demo runs. The piece focuses on production patterns rather than theoretical

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.900
limits of the underlying models. Teams moving agents into real environments should review the

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:45.140
checklist before scaling. The analysis draws directly from observed deployments rather than simulated

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:51.660
scenarios. Searches for prompt injection have doubled in the past year as more teams ship agents.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:58.140
The trend reflects rising awareness of a core agent reliability issue rather than any single

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:05.020
new attack. Developers should treat injection resistance as a first class evaluation criterion alongside

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:13.260
task success. The increase tracked on auto gpt.net tracks directly with the number of production agent

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:20.620
rollouts. No isolated exploit accounts for the full growth in search volume. Evaluation suites now

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:26.680
routinely test for injection vectors in addition to completion metrics. Okay, let's pop the hood on

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:33.180
sliding window key value cache compression. Everyone talks about key value cache compression as if

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:39.780
it is a simple just drop the unimportant token switch. In practice, Kara shows it is a sliding window

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:46.460
decode time policy that scores recent context with bidirectional attention and then uses a token

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:52.620
two chunk module to preserve flexible semantic chunks instead of fixed blocks. The threshold

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.980
triggered methods it replaces often create information holes or actually lower throughput.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:04.340
Kara avoids both by operating only on the active window and expanding selected pairs into

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:12.220
variable length chunks. The resulting Kav LLM implementation on top of VLLM delivers measurable memory

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:18.660
reduction and higher output throughput. But the gains depend on how well the bidirectional score matches

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:24.820
the model's actual attention patterns. The practical decision is straightforward. Use it when long

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:30.920
chain of thought traces are the bottleneck and you already run on paged attention. Skip it if your

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:36.800
workload stays short or if you cannot tolerate any risk of dropping a critical intermediate reasoning

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:44.260
step. Install web brain in Chrome or Firefox and test the act mode on a multi-step research workflow

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:52.520
using a local Olama model. Replicate Simon Willison CLI coding agent on top of the LLM library if

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:58.720
you already maintain Python tooling around model calls. Add the office comprehension bench to your

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:06.020
document agent evaluation harness to measure real file format understanding. Run p provenance guard on

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:11.460
an existing agent loop to compare misalignment detection rates against your current large language

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:18.740
model as a judge setup. More local first browser agents are expected as the web brain pattern spreads.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:25.640
Additional provenance style guardrails will likely appear as teams publish on misalignment benchmarks.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:31.960
Continued growth in prompt injection tooling and evaluation as agent deployments increase.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:38.620
Tomorrow keep an eye on further extensions to the ask and act modes in local browser agents.

